d in the  particular direction conversely if the  bike instead of going in this direction  went in the opposite direction - well of  measurement velocity in that direction  we have a minus velocity because  velocity has a direction so it would be  minus 10 meters per second  finally if this bike was going that way  it's neither going towards positive or  negative velocity so if it was going  either of that way or that way it would  actually have a velocity of 0 meters per  second even if its speed was 10 meters  per second because we're not going  towards positive or negative its  velocity would be 0 meters per second so  that's the difference between speed and  velocity speed has just the magnitude  does it matter upon the direction  whereas velocity has a magnitude and a  direction the other example I'm going to  talk through is the difference between  distance and displacement so let's  imagine for a minute that we had a 400  meter trap so here it is say it's a an  athletics event it's a circular track  that people run round for the 400 meters  whatever this person starts they run all  the way around here and they finish it  the exact point that they started sochi  distance is a scalar quantity if this  person is running the 400 meters the  distance is it doesn't matter upon the  direction at all it's just how much  ground are they're covered in total so  the distance for this person running  would be 400 meters at the very very end  of the race however the displacement is  the distance traveled in a straight line  from the starting point so if someone  has run all the way here and they  finished exactly where they started  their displacement is going to be zero  at the end because the distance from the  starting point is zero let's look at one  more example of how this works  let's imagine if I have this line here  that I'll draw so let's say it's 10  centimeters in that direction  and 5 centimeters in that direction  ooh centimeters and 5 centimeters there  so 